=== 1. TICKET NUMBERS: CONFIRMED, and the blast radius is larger ===

Reproduced exactly as written, both formats:
  0.1+0.2  digest 0.30000000000000004  emitted 0.3                self MAC FAIL, sops -d exit 51
  1/3      digest 0.3333333333333333   emitted 0.333333333333333  self MAC FAIL, sops -d exit 51

"all three JSON backends": confirmed at the encoder level -- Cpanel::JSON::XS, JSON::XS
and JSON::PP all render a pure NV 0.1+0.2 as 0.3. Since ADR 0005 the distribution only
ever uses Cpanel, so the backend choice is irrelevant to this defect, which is what
"survives ADR 0005" means.

Corrections / additions the ticket does not have:

(1a) THE TWO FORMATS ARE NOT SYMMETRIC. The trigger is a float SV with NOK and no POK.
     YAML::XS retains the PV of every float it parses (measured: plain, flow, in a
     sequence, anchored, aliased, nested -- all -NP) and emits that PV verbatim and
     unquoted. So a float that came out of a YAML document survives a YAML round trip
     by accident. Cpanel::JSON::XS returns a bare NV (-N-), so a float that came out of
     a JSON document does NOT.
     Consequence: rotate/encrypt_in_place on a LEGITIMATE sops-written JSON document
     destroys the value and produces a file the real sops rejects:
       sops -e writes  "sum_unencrypted": 0.30000000000000004   (sops -d exit 0)
       File::SOPS rotate rewrites it as 0.3                      (sops -d exit 51)
     YAML rotate of the same document is fine (exit 0). The ticket reads as if only
     self-produced documents are affected; JSON rotate of a foreign document is worse.

(1b) edit() SILENTLY CORRUPTS INSTEAD OF FAILING. Measured on a sops-written JSON
     document with an unencrypted 0.30000000000000004 and an editor that changes an
     unrelated key: edit returns 1, and the resulting file is accepted by sops -d
     (exit 0) -- with the value now 0.3. edit round-trips through the plaintext
     emitter, so the degraded value and the digest agree and nothing complains.
     This is the worst variant: no error anywhere, wrong number on disk.

(1c) decrypt_file AND extract LOSE PRECISION ON *ENCRYPTED* FLOATS, BOTH FORMATS.
     "only unencrypted floats are affected" is true of the MAC, not of the data.
     decrypt_value converts the plaintext with +0.0, giving a bare NV, which the
     plaintext emitter then writes at 15 digits:
       sops -d                 -> ratio: 0.30000000000000004
       File::SOPS decrypt_file -> ratio: 0.3
       sops -d --extract       -> 0.30000000000000004
       File::SOPS extract      -> 0.3
     decrypt_file is fixed by the same emitter change; extract is an API-lane matter
     (it hands the caller an NV, and any stringification of it loses the digits).

(1d) CROSS-FORMAT CONVERSION BREAKS EVEN FOR YAML OUTPUT: a tree parsed from JSON and
     emitted as YAML carries bare NVs, so the YAML document also fails (exit 51).
     Same for any caller-supplied computed float, e.g. encrypt(data => {x_unencrypted
     => 0.1+0.2}, format => 'yaml').

(1e) SIBLING DEFECTS IN THE SAME FAMILY, found while building the case matrix. All are
     "the emitter and value_to_bytes disagree about a float"; none is the 16/17-digit
     case, all should be separate tickets:
       YAML  -0.0     emitted 0     digest -0     sops -d exit 51, self MAC FAIL
       YAML  +Inf     emitted Inf   digest +Inf   sops -d exit 51 (self OK)
       JSON  NaN/Inf  emitted null  digest NaN    sops -d exit 51, self MAC FAIL
     (JSON -0.0 is fine today -- Cpanel writes -0.0, which is exactly what ADR 0005
     bought. Any fix must not regress it; see 3b.)

(1f) HOW OFTEN THIS FIRES. Hand-typed decimals essentially never need >15 significant
     digits (0 of 0.1 0.5 1.5 3.14 2.718 99.99 0.001 1e10 12.34). Computed values almost
     always do: 1/n for n=1..1000 -> 93%, n/7 -> 86%, rand() -> 92%, 0.01*n -> 13%.
     That is why a hand-written config never trips it and any machine-generated
     document does.

=== 2. OPTION (c) IS AN INTEROP REGRESSION, AND A LEAKY ONE ===

Prototype: assert_representable additionally croaks when sprintf('%.15g',$v)+0 != $v.
Measured against documents the real sops wrote:

  encrypt() of a float that WILL BE ENCRYPTED   CROAKS (yaml and json)
  decrypt of a sops-written document            OK  (assert is not on that path)
  rotate  of a sops-written document            CROAKS -- yaml AND json
  encrypt_in_place of the plaintext             CROAKS -- yaml AND json
  edit    of a sops-written json document       does NOT croak (see 1b: the value has
                                                already been degraded to 0.3 by then)

Three findings:

(2a) It refuses values that have a perfectly good SOPS wire form. An encrypted float is
     ENC[...,type:float] whose plaintext IS the canonical decimal; it round-trips through
     both implementations today. The int64 case is not analogous -- there no wire form
     preserves the value; here one does, and (c) would refuse it.

(2b) rotate on a legitimate sops-written document would croak in BOTH formats, where
     `sops -r` succeeds and where File::SOPS YAML rotate succeeds today. That is the
     answer to the question the ticket asks: yes, (c) is an interop regress.

(2c) (c) cannot be expressed at assert_representable at all. "Does not survive its own
     emitter" is a property of the emitter, and assert_representable sees only a scalar.
     A format-blind 15-digit rule refuses YAML documents that work today; a
     format-aware rule needs the emit-and-reparse check that option (a) needs. So any
     correct (c) is option (a) minus the substitution -- same machinery, no fix.

=== 3. OPTION (a) IS FEASIBLE FOR BOTH FORMATS. Prototyped end to end. ===

The requirement is weaker than "emit the canonical form": the MAC agrees iff the emitted
decimal PARSES BACK TO THE SAME DOUBLE, because Go re-derives FormatFloat(v,'f',-1,64)
from whatever it parsed. Verified: a sops document carrying the non-canonical but
round-tripping literal 0.300000000000000044408920985 verifies and rotates fine.

Per-emitter capability, measured:

  YAML::XS   dualvar(NV, canonical) -> emits the PV verbatim, UNQUOTED, as a real
             scalar. WORKS. No new dependency (Scalar::Util is core).
             Caveat: YAML::XS emits a dualvar's PV whatever it says -- dualvar(0.3,
             'hello') emits `v: hello`. It is a raw-text primitive with no guard rail,
             which is fine only because the text comes from value_to_bytes itself.
  YAML::PP   dualvar -> QUOTED ('0.30000000000000004'). Does not work.
             $ypp->schema->add_representer(class_equals => ..., code => sub { $node->
             {data} = $text; $node->{style} = YAML_PLAIN_SCALAR_STYLE }) DOES work.
             But that means replacing YAML::XS as the emitter -- ADR 0001's rejected
             option 2. YAML::PP does sort keys by default (checked), so the MAC's
             sorted-key premise survives, but it emits block scalars where YAML::XS
             emits one-line double-quoted strings, i.e. it moves the wire bytes of
             every multi-line string in every document. Not worth it when the dualvar
             works.
  Cpanel::JSON::XS  dualvar -> QUOTED. JSON_TYPE_FLOAT (Cpanel::JSON::XS::Type) ->
             0.3, no help. convert_blessed/TO_JSON -> quoted.
             allow_bignum + Math::BigFloat WORKS: {"v":0.30000000000000004} as a bare
             JSON number, exact text from BigFloat's bstr. Measured: allow_bignum
             changes nothing else on encode (byte-identical output for a sample with
             ints, NVs, strings, booleans, undef, arrays, -0.0). Must be a SEPARATE
             encoder instance -- allow_bignum on the shared object would also change
             decoding.
  JSON::XS   has no allow_bignum at all (confirmed: method does not exist). Irrelevant
             since ADR 0005 names Cpanel, but it means (a) is only available because
             the backend was pinned.
  JSON::PP   allow_bignum works too.
  post-pass over emitted text: possible in principle -- YAML::XS never emits a block
             scalar (checked with newlines, tabs, a 200-char string) and Cpanel pretty
             output is one value per line -- but it needs a text position -> tree leaf
             mapping, i.e. a parser built out of regexes. That is the exact defect
             class ADR 0001 removed. Rejected on fragility, not on feasibility.

