  Zero wire-byte movement for every value that works today, every broken 16/17-digit
  case fixed, and no new failures. That matrix is from the MINIMAL-INTERVENTION variant:
  render the scalar with the real emitter, reparse it, and substitute only when the
  emitter's own output does not come back as the same double. The naive always-wrap
  variant also fixes everything but moves bytes for values that are fine today
  (1e+20 -> 100000000000000000000, 1e-20 -> 0.00000000000000000001, JSON 1.0 -> 1) AND
  REGRESSES JSON -0.0 to exit 51, because Math::BigFloat->new('-0') drops the sign.
  Recommend the minimal variant; it needs no -0.0 carve-out because -0.0 fails the
  round-trip test on its own terms and is excluded by a sign check.

(3b) COSTS OF (a): a new runtime dependency on Math::BigFloat (core, JSON path only,
     one object per float leaf that needs it); the float canonicalisation pass lives in
     the two format handlers but its TEXT must come from Encrypted::value_to_bytes, so
     the single-source-of-truth rule holds -- the wrapper is a carrier, not a second
     conversion; the wrappers must be applied to a COPY of the tree and only AFTER the
     digest, because a Math::BigFloat leaf would make detect_type say 'str'; and NaN/
     Inf must be excluded (BigFloat renders them as JSON null, same as today).
     Lane note: the change sits in Format/YAML.pm and Format/JSON.pm but it moves the
     digest's agreement with the document, so per CLAUDE.md it is wire-lane work with a
     format-lane review, not the other way round.

(3c) BONUS, measured: (a) also fixes 1c's decrypt_file precision loss in both formats
     (plaintext output becomes 0.30000000000000004), because decrypt_file goes through
     the same emit(). extract is unaffected and stays an API-lane ticket.

=== 4. OPTION (b) IS OUT. Three independent reasons, one measured. ===

ADR 0001's boundary is explicit: "YAML::PP supplies order and nothing else... Nothing
YAML::PP produces reaches the digest as data, so a divergence in how it represents a
scalar cannot change a MAC." Option (b) makes the reparse's scalar representation the
digest's data source, which is the one thing that ADR forbids. That is not a
formality -- measured, YAML::XS (libyaml, YAML 1.1) and YAML::PP (YAML 1.2 core) resolve
7 of 29 tested YAML scalars to different values: 0o17 (0o17 vs 15), 0x1f (0x1f vs 31),
NULL and Null (string vs undef), .inf (.inf vs +Inf), .nan (.nan vs NaN), Inf (+Inf vs
Inf). Under (b) the digest would follow YAML::PP where sops follows yaml.v3. Booleans
and every float tested do agree, and for JSON documents Cpanel and YAML::PP agree on all
8 numeric literals tested -- but a fix that trades one wrong-scalar class for seven is
not a fix.

Second: on the encrypt side (b) is structurally awkward. The digest goes INTO the
document as sops.mac, so hashing the document requires emit -> reparse -> hash ->
re-emit. And for ENCRYPTED leaves the digest is over the plaintext, which is not in the
document at all, so (b) can only cover unencrypted leaves -- a digest with two sources,
which is the defect class this lane exists to prevent.

Third and decisive: (b) does not fix the bug, it ratifies it. If the digest covers what
the emitter wrote, File::SOPS stores 0.3 where the caller passed 0.30000000000000004,
the document verifies, and nothing reports anything. That is exactly the measured edit()
behaviour in 1b -- sops -d exit 0 with the wrong number on disk.

=== RECOMMENDATION (maintainer decides) ===

(a), in the minimal-intervention form, with an ADR before the code. It is the only
option that keeps the value the caller gave us; it moves no wire byte that works today;
it fixes decrypt_file as a side effect; and both emitters have a mechanism that
demonstrably works. (c) is a regression on both formats and cannot even be written where
the ticket puts it. (b) is excluded by ADR 0001 and by the fact that it would make the
corruption official.

